description:
MIP-3 sections 1-3 and 9. The first `muster-daemon` binary: it holds tabs and pane trees by Muster's names, spawns panes, and serves the control connection from a_2XJ1g2eBz. Pane byte streams, input, detection and persistence are separate cards that build on this.

In scope:
- one daemon per machine per install, on a socket named for that install, so a dev build never adopts the release daemon and tests never adopt either;
- the session model: tabs (Muster tab name, label with generation counter, pane tree with axis/ratio per split, zoomed pane), panes (name, cwd, command, title, alive), grouped-tab membership by tab name only. No workspace;
- spawning: the user's shell as an interactive login shell; a command runs as `$SHELL -l -i -c '<command>; exec $SHELL -l -i'`; the create request's grid sizes the PTY from birth; env adds `COLORTERM`, `MUSTER_PANE`, `MUSTER_SOCKET` (TERM, terminfo and shell integration are their own card); child reaping, SIGHUP to the process group on close, no fd leaks;
- the control connection: every a_2XJ1g2eBz request that does not need streams or detection, with events emitted before answers;
- the structure that handoff will need later (MIP-3 section 10): a pane can be rebuilt from a PTY master plus persisted fields, so nothing ties a pane's state to being the PTY's parent.

herdr references for PTY handling, if useful: `src/pty/actor/unix.rs`, `src/pane.rs` spawn path (~1635-2090), `src/platform/{macos,linux}.rs` for cwd reads (herdr v0.8.0, Apache-2.0).

Tests go through the neutral harness card and spawn the real binary.
